extra.final_message:
The build succeeds and the fix is in place.

## Diagnosis

The crash is an `ArithmeticException: divide by zero` in `TotpProgressBar.refresh()`:

```java
int maxProgress = getMax();          // 5000 (card_entry.xml) or 1000 (compact/small layouts)
int animPart = maxProgress / _period;  // 5000 / 9999 == 0
int animEnd = (currentProgress / animPart) * animPart;   // ← / by zero
```

The animation was written assuming the progress bar's `max` is a multiple of the period, i.e. that one second of the period is worth at least one unit of progress. `animPart` is "progress units per second" as *integer* division, so for any period larger than the bar's `max` (>1000 in the compact/small entry layouts, >5000 in the default one) it becomes `0` and the next line throws.

`setShowProgress()` → `_progressBar.start()` → `refresh()` runs on the UI thread while binding the entry, so the app crashed instantly and then crashed again on every launch once the 9999s entry was saved in the vault — exactly what the reporter described.

## Fix

Reworked `refresh()` to do the chunking in the **time domain** rather than in integer progress units, so it is period-independent:

- The chunk length is `clamp(1s … millisTillRotation)` but never shorter than the time it takes the bar to advance by one unit (`periodMillis / maxProgress`). This removes the `animPart` division entirely, and — importantly — also prevents the degenerate case where a naive `Math.max(animPart, 1)` guard would make `animEnd == currentProgress`, yielding a `postDelayed(…, 0)` busy-loop / ANR instead of a crash.
- `animEnd` is computed as the progress value at `now + chunk`, via a shared `calcProgress()` helper that clamps to `[0, max]` and uses `double`/`long` math (the old `float` + `_period * 1000` int expression was also overflow-prone).
- Guarded the `1000 / _animDurationScale` division against a scale of `0` (animations disabled in developer options), which previously produced a `Integer.MAX_VALUE` duration.
- `setPeriod()` now falls back to the default 30s for periods `TotpInfo.isPeriodValid()` rejects, and the static `TotpInfo.getMillisTillNextRotation(int)` does the same — it is called with `EntryAdapter.getMostFrequentPeriod()`, which returns `-1` when there is no uniform period group and previously produced a *negative* delay fed straight into `UiRefresher.postDelayed()`.

Note the period `9999` remains valid and usable, which matches the reporter's expectation ("new code should be generated when the entered time is up") rather than rejecting the input.

I verified the arithmetic by extracting the old and new math into a standalone harness and sweeping periods `{1, 5, 30, 60, 999, 1000, 1001, 9999, 2147483, 0, -1}` × max `{1000, 5000}`, stepping through a full rotation each time. The old code throws `/ by zero` for every period exceeding `max` (and for `0`) and yields negative progress for `-1`; the new code produces in-range, non-increasing progress with strictly positive re-post delays in all
...[truncated 4561 chars]...
Delayed(this::refresh, realAnimDuration);
+        // the animation only lasts for the duration of a single chunk, so restart it after that
+        _handler.postDelayed(this::refresh, Math.max(realAnimDuration, 1));
+    }
+
+    private static int calcProgress(int maxProgress, long periodMillis, long millisTillRotation) {
+        if (periodMillis <= 0) {
+            return 0;
+        }
+
+        int progress = (int) (maxProgress * ((double) millisTillRotation / periodMillis));
+        return Math.max(Math.min(progress, maxProgress), 0);
     }
 }
```

`./gradlew clean assembleDebug` completes successfully (only pre-existing deprecation/unchecked notes), producing `app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk`.

One caveat worth flagging: I could not produce a `git diff` for this — the project directory sits inside a parent repo where it is untracked, so the patch above was assembled by hand against the pre-edit file contents. The working tree itself holds the real, compiled change.
